rix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93af4894-4929-5ba8-9e52-45ac424912a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550738} :answer-field/id #uuid "a03b69e0-c573-57f3-bd3a-5e82ec61e1fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6f36bc2-1e9d-59b6-b406-9f6515dd271d" :question/math-academy-id "q-254908" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[2,\\,-7,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[-3,\\,-1,\\,5]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|2-(-3)|,|-7-(-1)|,|1-5|) \\\\  & =max(5,6,4) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354f21b2-96ab-5d34-8629-17b1cd8d23d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551791} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee3cf3b-86e3-5e57-83f5-b5451cd4c7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551893} {:answer/id #uuid "800e78fd-bc77-5f21-8b03-8b51fc29c534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551970} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ccdf0e-7384-5c78-bdab-6715b3b614a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552133} {:answer/id #uuid "f270c863-ecf9-5d37-bac9-47ebceb975b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551791} :answer-field/id #uuid "3231d9e1-5db0-532b-9d06-68eafedeb402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "226b9b94-926b-577f-834a-35ff659d1159" :question/math-academy-id "q-102338" :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a31093c-3bc1-5604-8b68-60bb29456071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551690} {:answer/id #uuid "1c236ae5-4aa5-5314-b3d7-d9f14364f42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}62 \\\\ 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} {:answer/id #uuid "55bb8ae9-83ab-5bd6-b3fa-7323cb3b3ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}62 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551870} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b3471b-58e2-538a-96d3-b94d24b16af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552101} {:answer/id #uuid "f9195545-d1e3-513f-91e6-f5228e90b375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} :answer-field/id #uuid "560cd508-cebe-5335-8021-8a9e70cdc673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47594296-da7d-5903-b008-c5f5265a8817" :question/math-academy-id "q-269489" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $$\n\\displaystyle L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)\n$$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 34\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}62 \\\\ 45\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30da5234-4042-5fab-bc17-28a88bfb9de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551781} {:answer/id #uuid "37e4fbe8-c0f7-58ed-9b71-06218e634007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551799} {:answer/id #uuid "868cbcd3-651b-5e03-a865-b0dfaf92d893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551989} {:answer/id #uuid "d0501560-e02a-5299-a647-3098cd11d592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c321cc-baeb-5602-885c-ed6669f5d604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6730a75f-f272-5493-a8bf-085f7b4def46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9669b6bb-a27d-584f-935a-b35adacb11c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-269476" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f2c097-7bd7-557a-a116-a684e091c780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} {:answer/id #uuid "591aca34-5191-5fe1-9db7-320758bc5e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553061} {:answer/id #uuid "a85a19cd-59c2-5d28-9c89-e1a6319ddd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553206} {:answer/id #uuid "c4582499-f706-5cc9-8e35-eb95cf03cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553258} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d4bacd-b2da-531e-8234-b3604fea9dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd3e00c-418a-520a-80fa-a7b949ac974e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a810631-1bae-5445-bc88-4aea289acecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335078" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfe2ae9-aec8-50cf-849c-115d91f5165f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552950} {:answer/id #uuid "786ae3e8-66e4-5326-b092-7941c56f8c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553113} {:answer/id #uuid "82a270b6-94b8-5f5f-8060-a6d5207f6169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2836-2dba-56ff-9d23-d8bc722d1e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} {:answer/id #uuid "fae32f0d-4f34-5b6e-8153-95de23dd818e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} :answer-field/id #uuid "9249f96b-64b8-59e4-9bf4-1d116673b015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b02e6de2-bb8c-5f1a-89b9-7e259d335937" :question/math-academy-id "q-335195" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6be835-40ec-54ab-80f9-e88f6e1c2e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553900} {:answer/id #uuid "58d46ea7-049e-5a25-a6c2-5ad53ae4d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554043} {:answer/id #uuid "a99fb21f-5f60-57d9-893f-48f42ff18616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554246} {:answer/id #uuid "d7117175-fba5-5e21-8e5f-4b0885591d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554337} {:answer/id #uuid "fd679fa5-f8b4-58d0-86cf-0a7829e9b639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554043} :answer-field/id #uuid "d228661a-466c-50ca-9003-6059a883594a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "555dd099-33d6-5089-b5ce-f831f111dabe" :question/math-academy-id "q-95363" :question/problem "$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}=1+2=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21856be1-b1fa-5f91-8a39-4aebb5209096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553926} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8f88fa-1943-5b66-9a8b-582f194c3e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554163} {:answer/id #uuid "9c971210-23ba-5a3d-9d48-b48f9a6fc7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554213} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c1e9fb-4917-514f-8d69-f634fa62d636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554289} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a9c01f-6c85-52fc-b668-f80c2e6a714d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554402} :answer-field/id #uuid "79a8ed53-1004-51ec-854e-1bc8377232da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af173c80-5734-52f5-887a-4f2d2e37a60e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37159" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259c4561-f4f6-5e22-b3d1-59bfa17cceb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555076} {:answer/id #uuid "28648a1d-d4da-54ce-9c6d-02600da5d23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555086} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0ab33a-2f17-5383-81d5-4116be86b3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555322} {:answer/id #uuid "93ebf0c4-eb4c-55d4-8425-5fd308be1a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555350} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555435} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94869" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064be7dc-c00e-551d-b59c-9b5c0cc3eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555009} {:answer/id #uuid "79fc7d50-2157-579c-9285-9ef0a16c6060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555283} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7e68b0-ecab-5c63-a1f5-236ebb590064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555412} {:answer/id #uuid "bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555449} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4bed5-b3f8-50de-9ea2-abc0ad3054b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555449} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-107535" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd98a66-67bb-5fc9-b62b-aeb298847393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556494} {:answer/id #uuid "76a7ec27-93f5-544c-9463-ed42ad6cb9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556698} {:answer/id #uuid "87c293eb-dc8e-51ca-a5f1-bbafb0c786eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556750} {:answer/id #uuid "b16396cd-321a-5d69-abaf-cebcedad9ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556865} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a8b92-03b1-5f6f-a205-b58ee49a644d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556865} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2882232-ab0e-5766-a29a-3d2353385bb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "196a5704-c0f5-5a90-b92b-f7fa820f51b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40131" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556469} {:answer/id #uuid "420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556577} {:answer/id #uuid "65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556664} {:answer/id #uuid "7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556722} {:answer/id #uuid "c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556664} :answer-field/id #uuid "9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32723" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ M & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556531} {:answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} {:answer/id #uuid "840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556740} {:answer/id #uuid "c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556908} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75098" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011655a2-c520-5cd0-a439-defc348c17b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558974} {:answer/id #uuid "48815a36-e11f-5e50-85f2-1df30f980d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558980} {:answer/id #uuid "91e08695-6405-567c-8d4e-0b8f3347a5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558986} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bde4ca-48f5-5c86-ad8e-88083b8a8731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558987} {:answer/id #uuid "ed23ef92-0aad-5b95-947e-ba7002f159c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558987} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a31c4b-f831-5da9-8f29-1b931744a7c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ce03692-58d4-5830-8d97-d77704c638fb" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,3 u + 5 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{9+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{14}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b115ea7-8c75-582e-bcb9-c63407864ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{16 v + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558975} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbc89bf-4b41-500d-a74f-a5a23cc77a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4 u^{2} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558978} {:answer/id #uuid "43feaff8-ab49-5cc0-a22e-c79061a43457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558979} {:answer/id #uuid "733b93c8-4090-5697-9f6e-1b3b25a0447f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{- 4 u^{2} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558984} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f43bf4-274b-5f91-890a-212b06e6e8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} \\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558979} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf6d408-766c-507b-a6fd-42b33e06fe2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c17b6edc-1f0e-51cb-a0ce-11f93aba061f" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle 2 u^{2},\\,u - v^{2},\\,v^{2}\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 4 u,\\,1,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 0,\\,- 2 v,\\,2 v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(2 v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{128 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2} \\left(32 u^{2} + 1\\right)} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}}\\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1} \\\\  &=2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{v^2}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D 2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cee569-5cea-5d57-9bc3-3903a71989af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558973} {:answer/id #uuid "0f83c76d-ec99-5241-aebf-9c4270e1a7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558976} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce6fda6-b951-5d87-8a85-6283f433ac85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558982} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1295a6-0f95-51f3-8fd1-ee96277ead12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558990} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1a18a-411a-5224-a056-02a4076cbe0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558991} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ffdcbfe-e3c8-554c-90ac-7c7fa5fdd703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f12a3689-dba6-56d9-843d-c0f7bf0ac72a" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,5 u + 7 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,5\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,7\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(5\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{25+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{30}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058e5939-7dca-5207-a9cf-7578532c3f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560302} {:answer/id #uuid "11a957d3-49ef-56db-ab0f-c02857167b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560304} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef0898a-8678-542f-ad45-70587f8bbd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\left(52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560309} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5c61b9-0f4d-5229-83c2-28dbe2298a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560314} {:answer/id #uuid "9f011f25-a217-5291-b94d-c478fcc9478a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560337} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2fc28a-c600-58d0-b8a2-e9ebeb14d152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b4808cc-728c-55b7-90f8-734ae2943c4e" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=3 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 2 \\\\ 2 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -3 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(4 u + 8 v^{2})^{2}+(6 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e2969c-7bc4-5828-b311-0b75c71bdde5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} 