ent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 3 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 3 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(3 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - 3 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=3 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =u v-3\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b418aa-8f50-529b-a254-a52c443915a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560306} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93004a-cd83-5a5e-91b3-6afd517b9553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\left(40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560334} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0da7f6-74c9-595e-935f-fa2d5e17164f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560339} {:answer/id #uuid "d3279af3-c719-5f48-8397-3cee94b39b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560341} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb763ec-517c-56ab-a78d-f970a030bcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560339} :answer-field/id #uuid "27483cd1-fc63-57b7-82c5-fd8540038450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d206b38-df94-5588-845c-04813c06e146" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- u,\\,3 u - v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-1,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 3 \\\\ 2 u & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -1 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 v)^{2}+(6 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(2 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=3 u - v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbbf8c5-b186-50e3-83eb-a34ac7c0c068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560316} {:answer/id #uuid "361ee39b-048e-5ba6-8456-29b75f167d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560319} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e9e0f-f4d1-5f9b-b01e-4c51ccc96d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560321} {:answer/id #uuid "71ff934d-be68-5886-a0e6-ceea28cda382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560327} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c64255-656c-592c-8b6f-d29d8add1e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\left(45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30326ca-d748-58e4-a5fd-30f7dcf9dd69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9b0c0134-9edd-51ff-b844-c0de83e1869b" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3 u v,\\,- u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 v,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 v & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & 2 \\\\ 3 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & -1 \\\\ 3 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 v)^{2}+(6 u + 12 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=3 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d456bb7-3a9d-56dd-93b0-7a184a9a4649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560303} {:answer/id #uuid "23028f52-cd66-57e0-afed-a3ec494e3381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560311} {:answer/id #uuid "7194190b-9e89-5f7e-af91-ce99d3f01169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560326} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc0030a-0473-5ca6-886a-e1f5a05cd61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\left(13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560335} {:answer/id #uuid "fe35efa1-95dd-5d26-a0cd-9af6226ea03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f22210f-68bc-50f9-9ee4-c533602be0ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d4bdd51-e427-51d6-919e-7d7980cc59de" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - 2 y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(2 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - 2 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-2\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c5ae1a-9e7a-5d8b-ab09-dd7199e67922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} {:answer/id #uuid "71c1cca6-df0f-5b2c-a21a-33d4064bd36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561867} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3cea84-cf2c-5de8-8b3e-ec044d0938a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561930} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8d20b-17c6-5858-b1f2-709c110bbb7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561967} {:answer/id #uuid "f454b9f0-465f-5b23-8ad6-a4f87f6b3abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaf5f5a-9372-5c9a-b844-dc81456ba76a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ca0b310-c54f-553d-a3d3-7e3881bc581e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95215" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R))=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07714214-c4d9-5b08-a2c8-6c1f7e8ec203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561605} {:answer/id #uuid "1eea31bf-e3b1-5614-bfbd-d7f54109fa12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561665} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc27688-e270-59b6-bbc0-9254bca9da7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561937} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e18e7f-729e-5ce5-b8db-386712a47470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562044} {:answer/id #uuid "eccebf2d-98a8-5ea0-a876-f56acb406b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562175} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd01ea1f-a466-5fd9-8311-a2132252bc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43503f70-a039-563c-bedc-d1b2782aedca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106884" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R))=4\\cdot 9=36.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562164} :answer-field/id #uuid "250a0410-56cf-50a6-9de1-7caaa7d5a608" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa32fab1-7f40-54da-a1e0-f1997cc223c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a725e18-61fa-5fa6-82d0-ff96754212ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9841f45c-6a19-51eb-88e2-92efb70ca08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561957} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc31d784-bb6f-5691-9bf1-b7d09bc77e17" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b8037b-0482-5919-b25b-6c561eb8d3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c78abd9f-d0b3-531a-96cd-b8ea8b5d6311" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561912} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18814a4-c3b7-50c0-86f8-c7a6e058383a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59f14881-731e-52d2-9146-dab6e581e8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253868" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aca304-7b48-53d3-b89a-9ec1254a925a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561589} {:answer/id #uuid "77982fef-cbd9-5b64-9beb-aef93c81e8b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561878} {:answer/id #uuid "bb24ab63-f2c2-5312-9f4a-1f89c660eb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562063} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cf2b8d-50b4-5dea-8cdb-f6d96e285ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562085} {:answer/id #uuid "e9436cfd-df38-58b3-bdb8-5acac9079d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561878} :answer-field/id #uuid "167770ed-e7de-54b6-9bfa-73c5e3df3786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b8162a3-2a18-5498-a3a6-ab4fdf90d980" :question/math-academy-id "q-105158" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $5a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-2x & =b \\\\ -2x & =b-5a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(b-5a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645b2e7b-ed0a-5176-a522-b400791972fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561828} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d7e4db-ae01-5275-b4c8-0fd8386be685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562002} {:answer/id #uuid "b27b7ca7-3a76-56c0-845b-41bdb8aea4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562028} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83f83-0973-5661-b724-fbd23032d35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562134} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a757e4-19f6-50f1-9887-1b2bae99eff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69d9c07-c486-5d96-be8f-04bbd335d396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7efa12c8-5625-54e7-8464-2de1d85a3ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95218" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ the vector space of all $7\\times 7$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48724e40-2537-5993-a9ac-f90136d4706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561765} {:answer/id #uuid "704ca1d5-8fbc-545a-a7fc-7fef0adaebcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561860} {:answer/id #uuid "ac081ada-e903-5cd6-86dc-983aac36c0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562010} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eef5a1-6369-528c-8470-5e884104b19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562153} {:answer/id #uuid "f989d214-29fe-5513-93bb-4bb8188098f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562153} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee18a9e-3a2d-5f93-9c78-89a367b51148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ec0dc53-4341-5ee6-84a6-d12231a31e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-106875" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 1$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nThus, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R)$ contains $5\\cdot 1=5$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ we have, indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R))=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a396a767-5b66-5bd2-8cc1-072e21a93b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563315} {:answer/id #uuid "a7554a6f-0aea-5a5a-9c28-e56e4d345ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563324} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d6b95a-ada9-5465-b9fb-5a72172eca4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563346} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fb20f9-8aa7-571a-9473-7ce075be4841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563450} {:answer/id #uuid "f06540a4-643a-5da8-a809-4757dd6f0a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563346} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dbde469-aa7e-5bcb-a66c-2dae3c6fa5e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6199f517-7214-5d4f-b510-e7e4d600a748" :question/math-academy-id "q-130211" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2100df4f-3a3b-55cc-a852-f5c980aced39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563019} {:answer/id #uuid "343f0047-5e45-5525-a95d-3d82e6459514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563063} {:answer/id #uuid "94d0189d-b6e8-5ec0-91a7-24c6aa1126d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563276} {:answer/id #uuid "c8baf397-fb31-54b6-9eeb-ba95d1df79e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563387} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7e9cb6-5c59-5664-9b12-adadc37bf320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563063} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84f65d5-7aae-5814-8581-c9fd5f811b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86cd099b-e0a6-5dec-b63e-d3e60dda2214" :question/math-academy-id "q-69728" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206b7aff-d2d1-5f6a-add6-b653f58dc164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563018} {:answer/id #uuid "60f53833-01a7-51b3-b367-c22fe50326ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563170} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d74bba-4689-5ebb-920d-944005be742c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563307} {:answer/id #uuid "c5495620-9d83-5275-9d56-bcfc0407808d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563379} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f6b4d0-5d37-52d1-8795-c9a0b9d298d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563379} :answer-field/id #uuid "a738fbe9-b746-555f-a431-6ff37c754328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a567a48-e070-587a-94df-8a2d7148a763" :question/math-academy-id "q-32976" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6bd69d-eb28-52f2-9e31-c5b78c1f5326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570980} {:answer/id #uuid "7df978ee-1cba-5e6d-a0df-7a5d2c0faa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571088} {:answer/id #uuid "b58b8460-eabb-5831-9222-ae5190c87747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571171} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b666c4-2e1e-5e88-95b7-208ffe3d3b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571247} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9e80fa-761b-575b-8358-1633af610880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2984758c-d2d0-5408-9558-da3f2d0bbdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f805a2f-454d-5b6b-b94e-f4a78a778d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-131099" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}-10\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-10{C}_{14} \\\\  & =-10\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10(-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =10(0-4(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)+0) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c409eaf-a88b-52d0-93e4-e06a7fbd8275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570931} {:answer/id #uuid "460aef30-08cb-5a20-8328-3ae1a8f946cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571000} {:answer/id #uuid "929a7047-4cab-559a-b902-fb43e8ec1d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571119} {:answer/id #uuid "e1597bd3-5f9c-5dd4-9a8d-382a63c04f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571237} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b70f72-82d4-57c3-b86c-4d54f9b46376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571000} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe34648-d2b0-538a-b862-47f017b7fd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d888b8b4-33e2-5465-96a7-f2cf43e527f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32746" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(W) & ={w}_{11}{C}_{11}+{w}_{12}{C}_{12}+{w}_{13}{C}_{13}+{w}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+5\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{11}+5{C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & ∗ & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5(2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =10(3\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2628d9-2ef7-52ba-ae71-6150e6f2287d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572928} :answer-field/id #uuid "68889a3d-e5ee-5d54-9a14-6cd62d10349e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd34578d-2535-5f34-83c6-c3e44442de3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573147} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d3976d-329b-5f5a-af1a-743745fa1745" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5042d60d-1d74-5a97-ae4c-6f45b8b23e98" :question/math-academy-id "q-318151" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}) & =2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b0dd0c-9d63-5a70-95a6-dd376b0a0954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572926} :answer-field/id #uuid "b37d9201-7dcf-5a89-a52b-32dccf01ca00" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83567ae1-8427-57f7-8486-981e82916dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572994} :answer-field/id #uuid "beab0d67-6115-58a0-9692-4c292e4251e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df07f0e4-b8c0-55c8-a0ad-afd25f375b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-318154" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\hspace{0.167em}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}) & =4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4db8f3-59c2-52bd-bbc6-7b8fc5889531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574973} {:answer/id #uuid "423e1384-7818-5410-95a8-d0d39c518bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575063} {:answer/id #uuid "6c462f0d-0366-5361-9779-d3c8686baa44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575145} {:answer/id #uuid "a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575242} {:answer/id #uuid "f4920f18-c1a5-58fd-9b3e-acfe48313d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575242} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c1e3e7-b7eb-5780-ac9f-e4101be00453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-107114" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b,c\\in R\\}.$    \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an element of $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =k\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a & 2b \\\\ 2c & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under scalar  multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5feb286-8b23-57c0-ac21-fed05b381fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581360} :answer-field/id #uuid "07597190-ac59-5428-a2ee-1bedde5eb782" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b80b34-c5f9-50a3-ba76-9ee7ae35e9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581218} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0bc86d1-6571-5da2-b952-6dc97f4ae7d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33cf76c5-c3a5-5b77-b26a-87104b2a8407" :question/math-academy-id "q-350410" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25px\">$⊙$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$-1$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$4$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a302b5c4-0461-5009-8491-f7db4cd6ec71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581322} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c01a3c-37b3-5f5f-87ea-cd4fb3a3bcec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581505} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e70a962-f692-51d3-a93b-f8ebbf0bddc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581276} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b79a7b-7f6b-543d-a8a3-27df8b85dc6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d4fa35-1579-5790-bbdc-96ca35065f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581353} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37b5d3e-3e0d-53e7-8116-4f3125d28473" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39b8d597-d04f-59be-b981-2d4d51fa5c59" :question/math-academy-id "q-350407" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$3$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25px\">$⊙$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523485e8-11e1-592f-85b0-00b08fc0a408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585323} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa3179c-4b19-5e60-91b5-26f9bf98f6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585355} {:answer/id #uuid "79c6ec69-d733-5d09-a84c-fce18f453c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585394} {:answer/id #uuid "c91b3930-4b4b-571f-9715-6dd69beb7bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585518} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a72a55-761b-54bc-b1b6-24849696e7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585355} :answer-field/id #uuid "434c9117-8247-5029-ac1d-1d3a605e81e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "451265f5-e2bb-5604-8977-cb1160820683" :question/math-academy-id "q-106846" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d611de-4cf1-5bac-8543-03bc6cd035a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591821} {:answer/id #uuid "1613713d-3d9d-580a-9af1-50efd6d73a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591822} {:answer/id #uuid "17f92c96-54e0-5fd8-a6da-49db6161509d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591826} {:answer/id #uuid "44e1af84-83ce-5d10-b965-19718d8b8cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591884} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac09189-146b-5988-bf68-ba01646a487b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591884} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb94533f-d5c1-5e8b-82d2-efb3fb33faf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "51661d74-6629-571a-8a09-af0a0ec03e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-70439" :question/problem "Let $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $q=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $3p-(q+2r).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3p-(q+2r),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-(q+2r) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e009ed-e38f-5f3f-b506-88fb6f0c9a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594367} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8c2ecb-71fd-5d9d-a8fe-54672a42986c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594380} {:answer/id #uuid "60b4d3f0-01fb-5268-a09e-429775a1fc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594422} {:answer/id #uuid "80552fbd-6f46-5dcf-88a8-c56e7d8de874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594455} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf6ccc5-5d21-58fb-8af0-8f752aebe818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594380} :answer-field/id #uuid "3600e22d-0d76-5c25-b1ba-f39e7596b6f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39d5931f-416f-5897-adc4-9cd73edc3ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40661" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={14}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2f89bc-4263-53b3-a909-37a5a882978d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594417} {:answer/id #uuid "77bdd868-7eca-50b2-8540-a32a46a4fa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594447} {:answer/id #uuid "999b7aa8-d034-5e5e-8e2f-f39250364dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ does not contain the zero matrix $O$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594480} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cf0f47-f015-5e95-a44a-63c22bfe741d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "there exists a matrix $A\\in Z$ such that $A+B\\ne O$ for any $B\\in Z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594563} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b0c5f8-8d89-5a74-a4a9-502ea0b1df4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594447} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e18283-458f-52ba-b0db-921afa180c14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64ce04e1-24f2-5731-a9fd-09168e6ae104" :question/math-academy-id "q-106814" :question/problem "Let $Z$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because **addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nin $Z.$ Then,\n\n$$\ndet(A+B)=det(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHence, $A+B\\notin Z.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in $Z$ since $det(-A)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot det(A)=1\\cdot 0=0,$ and $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ multiplication by a scalar $\\alpha \\in R$ is closed since $det(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.$ Hence, $\\alpha A\\in Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Multiplication of matrices with real entries by a scalar is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6de01b-679a-5baa-9a9d-81da8f26e179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594361} {:answer/id #uuid "1f98e5d8-5a5c-5242-a16b-f58df045d0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594364} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5b24fe-9f45-5e26-ace1-4fff3693fa5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594468} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c0c59a-28f9-535b-8397-486d864cfb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594486} {:answer/id #uuid "dee383af-ca45-5d80-9267-bca2474ee066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594468} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb0e942-8154-5842-9cd7-b9f0e6c530a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8eb2b866-11c6-531d-8934-8f7819ddc747" :question/math-academy-id "q-124275" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u+3v+1,\\,y=3u+2v-2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-3y-8,\\,v=by+cx+13,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+3v+1 \\\\ 3u+2v-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+3v \\\\ 3u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-1)+(-3)\\cdot (y+2) \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (x-1)+5\\cdot (y+2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y-8 \\\\ 5y-3x+13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-3y-8,\\,v=5y+(-3)x+13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+5+(-3)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8b4298-844c-564c-9fab-4681da466ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594343} {:answer/id #uuid "30c39fc7-cff2-5154-9ca7-5086eb270961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594386} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5771f8-790c-5446-bfed-af77c825cee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594418} {:answer/id #uuid "8224e886-7c4c-54cb-a127-70dc78ac962b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594457} {:answer/id #uuid "eb890903-6220-58b5-87af-e7a9a4935482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594386} :answer-field/id #uuid "d591aacf-2752-506a-9108-d2b38a8989d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6353c7d-5c28-59f2-8ec7-3961a7f70bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124222" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 6 \\\\ -4 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-5)-6\\cdot (-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+5+8=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dcc281-2954-553e-85df-b2f0a146b6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596115} {:answer/id #uuid "5b63239f-68b4-56e6-908b-40798eb8c95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596225} {:answer/id #uuid "67c91ea2-823f-55f4-ad73-17c18ecb13e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596249} {:answer/id #uuid "872cf26f-b537-5f5a-b6b4-14bd0df480ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596296} {:answer/id #uuid "89c446c9-afb0-567e-8891-168dda7e77ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596249} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de0975b-c189-5b10-aee7-198b7786f2cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "063aeb47-cc32-5866-89d5-e236fc5f0c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-74980" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43282c-0cf0-5b3e-8a8a-ad676b6134c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596100} {:answer/id #uuid "14511788-1583-59d2-b77f-e7cd0e3d763c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596111} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0978f6-2a63-5dec-9711-3250e7457b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596119} {:answer/id #uuid "22b42229-2e2d-5606-bf7e-90c340abf4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596133} {:answer/id #uuid "6c20b417-687c-5436-a128-a05c0b007a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596256} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd2731ad-3315-5e8a-9ee3-66123d65a523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08036111-0c20-583b-9b87-91af757f0cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-110757" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=-3\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$"}]}
